  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/hypostomus_ancistroides.htm",
      "html": "<ul><li>Conservation status: Least Concern (LC) (IUCN 2022)</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/hypostomus_ancistroides_en-2/",
      "html": "<p>Origin: Paraguay.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/hypostomus_ancistroides.htm",
      "html": "<p>South America: Tietê River basin, Ribeira de Iguape River basin, Brazil. Type locality: Rio Tatuhy, left-bank tributary of the Rio Sorocaba, Rio Piracicaba, State of São Paulo [Brazil].</p><p>Hypostomus ancistroides is widely distributed, frequent, and abundant. Since no significant threats to its population have been detected, it has been listed in the Least Concern (LC) category (IUCN 2022).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/hypostomus_ancistroides.htm",
      "html": "<p>One of the smallest in this genus, it would make a good aquarium inhabitant in a large aquarium. As this genus is in the main messy eaters, good water quality and filtration need to be maintained. A lower water temperature is advised for this species.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/hypostomus_ancistroides_en-2/",
      "html": "<p>This is a subtropical species and can therefore be kept in an unheated indoor tank.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/hypostomus_ancistroides.htm",
      "html": "<p>Will eat all manner of foods in the aquarium: vegetable foods, tablets, pellets and good quality flake.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/hypostomus_ancistroides.htm",
      "html": "<p>Description: Body deep; greatest body depth contained 4.2 to 4.7 and caudal peduncle depth 9.8 to 10.0 times in SL. Mouth inferior. Ground colour brown, with dark-brown blotches on the body, especially on the dorsal region. Fins dark brown with black spots.</p><p>Etymology: The specific name ancistroides is Neo-Latin from eidos, form or shape, referring to its resemblance to some species of Ancistrus.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/hypostomus_ancistroides_en-2/",
      "html": "<p>Suggestion of a common name: Whiptail pleco.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/hypostomus_ancistroides.htm",
      "html": "<p>Burgess, W.E., 1989. An atlas of freshwater and marine catfishes. A preliminary survey of the Siluriformes. T.F.H. Publications, Inc., Neptune City, New Jersey (USA). 784 p.</p><p>Chico Mendes Institute for Biodiversity Conservation (ICMBio). 2022. Hypostomus ancistroides. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2022.</p><p>Ferraris, C.J. Jr., 2007. Checklist of catfishes, recent and fossil (Osteichthyes: Siluriformes), and catalogue of siluriform primary types. Zootaxa 1418:1-628.</p><p>Froese, R. and D. Pauly. Editors. 2020. FishBase. World Wide Web electronic publication. www.fishbase.org, (02/2020).</p>"
    }
